---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import AirportPageHeader from '../components/AirportPageHeader.astro';
import AppealLetter from '../components/AppealLetter.astro';
import AirportSidebar from '../components/AirportSidebar.astro';

const title = "Appeal Your Gatwick Airport Drop-Off Charge | Free PCN Appeal Letter 2026";
const description = "Got a parking charge notice for dropping off at Gatwick? Use our free appeal letter template to challenge your Gatwick PCN. Covers North and South Terminal.";
const canonical = "https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/gatwick-drop-off-charge-appeal/";

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "How to Appeal a Gatwick Airport Drop-Off Charge",
  "description": "Step-by-step guide and free appeal letter for challenging a Gatwick Airport drop-off PCN, covering both North and South Terminal.",
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealPCN" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN.co.uk" },
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much is the Gatwick Airport drop-off charge?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Gatwick currently charges £5 for a standard drop-off, enforced via ANPR cameras at both North and South Terminal." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I appeal a Gatwick drop-off charge?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. Gatwick's drop-off charges are issued by a private parking company, not the council. You have 28 days to submit a formal appeal. If it is rejected, check the letter for the independent appeals service and deadline. Times24 letters may ask for photo ID before giving a decision." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the Gatwick drop-off charge apply to both terminals?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, both the North and South Terminal at Gatwick have dedicated drop-off charge zones, each enforced by ANPR cameras. The boundaries and signage differ slightly between the two." }
    },
  ],
};

const tips = [
  { heading: '28-day deadline', body: "Don't miss it, after 28 days you lose the formal right to appeal at the first stage." },
  { heading: 'Specify your terminal', body: 'Always state North or South Terminal in your appeal. The zones and signage differ between them.' },
  { heading: 'Request ANPR images', body: 'Ask for the entry and exit photos in your letter. Timing errors are common and hard for operators to defend.' },
  { heading: 'Check the appeal service', body: "If rejected, follow the service and deadline in your letter. Times24 letters may offer a free IAS appeal, but check the risks first." },
  { heading: 'Send by tracked post', body: "Proof of delivery matters if the case escalates." },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  currentPage="gatwick"
  schema={[articleSchema, faqSchema]}
>

  <main>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="main-content">
        <div class="content-grid">

          <AirportSidebar current="Gatwick Airport" tips={tips} sticky />

          <div class="airport-main-column">
            <AirportPageHeader
              title="Appeal Your Gatwick Airport Drop-Off Charge"
              description="Charged for dropping someone off at Gatwick's North or South Terminal? You may have strong grounds to appeal, and our free template takes the guesswork out of the process."
              airport="Gatwick"
              integrated
            />
            <AppealLetter airport="Gatwick Airport">
              <p>I appeal as the registered keeper of the vehicle. I am not obliged to identify the driver and decline to do so.</p>
              <p>1. Times Parking cannot hold the registered keeper liable for this charge. The land is under statutory control, including the Gatwick Airport byelaws made under section 63 of the Airports Act 1986 [and Gatwick Airport Limited's traffic regulation orders]. It is therefore not "relevant land" under paragraph 3(1)(c) of Schedule 4 to the Protection of Freedoms Act 2012 ("POFA"). The POFA keeper liability provisions do not apply, and Times Parking has no lawful basis to pursue the registered keeper.</p>
              <p>2. This charge is not a byelaw penalty. It is a contractual parking charge, which can only be claimed from the driver. If the airport wished to enforce against keepers under its byelaws, that would be a matter for the airport. Times Parking does not own the airport and has not said it acts under the byelaws. The Private Parking Code of Practice says operators must make clear whether they are enforcing a byelaw or a contractual obligation (Annex D), and that nothing in the Code overrules byelaws.</p>
              <p>Under clause 8.1.3, the operator must not presume that the registered keeper was driving.</p>
              <p>3. The charge was not brought to the driver's attention before the contract was formed. Under Thornton v Shoe Lane Parking [1971] 2 QB 163, terms that are not brought to the other party's attention before the contract is made do not bind them. Times Parking must show that the charge was clearly displayed before the vehicle entered the zone.</p>
              <p>4. The signage does not comply with section 3 of the Private Parking Code of Practice. In particular:<br>
                - There was no entrance sign showing that terms apply and whether payment is required (3.1.1 to 3.1.2 and Annex A).<br>
                - The charge was not shown (3.1.3(j)) or was not in a legible font (3.1.4).<br>
                - The signs could not be read on approach or in the lighting conditions.<br>
                Without clear terms shown before the driver committed to entering the zone, no valid contractual offer was made. Times Parking is put to strict proof, with dated photographs, of the signs in place at the time.</p>
              <p>The registered keeper cannot be presumed or inferred to have been the driver, nor pursued under any interpretation of agency law. The Notice to Keeper can only hold the driver liable. Any reliance on Elliott v Loake would not assist: that was a criminal case decided on forensic and other evidence, not a presumption that the keeper was driving. Times Parking has produced no evidence of who was driving.</p>
              <p>Please refrain from asking for a drivers license, I am not obliged to send it and refuse to do so. Please also refrain from attempting to use Elliott v Loake as a valid argument.</p>
              <p>Elliott v Loake (1982) is a criminal case where the court concluded the registered keeper of a car was also the driver because physical and forensic evidence proved it, not because a keeper is automatically presumed to be the driver.</p>
              <p>I require this PCN to be cancelled and ask for written confirmation.</p>
             </AppealLetter>

            <div class="instructions-card">
              <h2>How to use this appeal template</h2>
              <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text">Click <strong>"Copy to clipboard"</strong> above, the full appeal is copied to your clipboard.</div></div>
              <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text">Log into the operator's online appeal system (or open your PCN) and paste the template into the appeal text box.</div></div>
              <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text">Submit and wait for a decision. The operator must respond within 35 days.</div></div>
              <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text">If rejected, check the letter for the independent appeal service and deadline. Times24 rejection letters may offer an IAS appeal. Read <a href="/guides/driving-licence-request/">our guide to parking companies asking for driving licences</a> before deciding what to do.</div></div>
            </div>

            <div class="airport-info-panel">
            <section class="intro-section">
              <h2>Gatwick drop-off charge appeal</h2>
              <p>Got a PCN for dropping someone off at Gatwick? Use the free template above. It covers both terminals.</p>

              <h3>The charge</h3>
              <p>Gatwick charges at the forecourts outside both the North and South Terminals. The rates are:
              <ul>
                <li>£10 for up to 10 minutes.</li>
                <li>£1 for each extra minute, up to 20 minutes.</li>
                <li>A maximum charge of £30.</li>
                <li>A maximum stay of 30 minutes.</li>
              </ul>
              </p>
              <p>You pay online or by phone, by midnight the day after.</p>
              <p>Use Gatwick's own website to pay. <a href="/guides/fake-airport-pcn-payment-sites/">Learn how to spot fake airport payment sites.</a></p>

              <h3>How you get a PCN</h3>
              <p>You don't pay by midnight the next day. Gatwick says late payment can also lead to a PCN.</p>
              <p>The PCN is £100. It drops to £60 if paid within 14 days.</p>

              <h3>Who runs it</h3>
              <p>Times Parking took over management of the terminal drop-off zones and related areas at London Gatwick Airport in late 2025.</p>
              <p>This is following a corporate name change and transition from NCP,which fell into administration <strong>for a second time</strong> in September 2026 after previously collapsing in March.</p>

              <h3>If they ask for a licence</h3>
              <p>Times24 have begun denying claims, requesting drivers licenses, and later incorrectly citing Elliott v Loake. If this happens to you, <a href="/guides/driving-licence-request/">we have a guide</a> on how to respond.</p>
              <p>We have amended the appeal to try and stop these attempts before they ask.</p>
              <p>They backtrack and claim that they are not relying on POFA, but on Elliott v Loake (1982) to assess who was driving. Not only does this contradict their earlier reply – it is also flawed, since Elliott v Loake is a criminal case in which other forensic evidence supported the conclusion that the registered keeper was the driver. That does not apply here.</p>

              <h3>Blue Badge</h3>
              <p>Blue Badge holders are exempt, but only if they register in advance. The exemption covers a 30-minute free drop-off.</p>

              <h3>Free alternative</h3>
              <p>You can drop off or pick up passengers at the long-stay car parks with two hours free parking and a free shuttle bus to the terminals.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="grounds-section">
              <details class="sources-section">
                <summary>Sources</summary>
              <ul>
                <li>Gatwick, <strong>Drop-off Charges</strong>: <a href="https://www.gatwickairport.com/transport-options/drop-off.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.gatwickairport.com/transport-options/drop-off.html</a></li>
                <li>Gatwick, <strong>Drop-off Terms and Conditions</strong>: <a href="https://www.gatwickairport.com/transport-options/drop-off/drop-off-terms-and-conditions.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.gatwickairport.com/transport-options/drop-off/drop-off-terms-and-conditions.html</a></li>
                <li>Gatwick, <strong>Regulation and Byelaws</strong>: <a href="https://www.gatwickairport.com/company/about-us/regulation.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.gatwickairport.com/company/about-us/regulation.html</a></li>
                <li>Gatwick Airport FAQs <strong>(£100 PCN, £60 within 14 days; Blue Badge exemption; 30-minute session)</strong>: <a href="https://www.gatwickairport.com/To_and_From_the_Airport" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.gatwickairport.com/To_and_From_the_Airport</a></li>
                <li>Protection of Freedoms Act 2012, <strong>Schedule 4</strong>: <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2012/9/schedule/4" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2012/9/schedule/4</a></li>
                <li>POPLA, <strong>Appeal guide</strong>: <a href="https://www.popla.co.uk/appeal-guide" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://www.popla.co.uk/appeal-guide</a></li>
              </ul>
              </details>
            </section>

            </div>

            <div class="disclaimer">
              <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> AppealAirportPCN.co.uk is a free information resource for UK drivers. Nothing on this site constitutes legal advice. Templates are provided as a starting point, always review your specific circumstances. For complex cases, consider consulting a solicitor or Citizens Advice.
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

</BaseLayout>
